Guo Zhonglian v. Qingdao Municipal Health Bureau and Qingdao Eastern Hospital — loan contract dispute

Plaintiff: Guo Zhonglian, male, 50 years old, manager of Qingdao Bacon International Trade Co., Ltd., Shandong Province, residing at Nanjing Road, Qingdao.

Defendant: Qingdao Municipal Health Bureau, Shandong Province. Domicile: Minjiang Road, Qingdao. Legal Representative: Liu Zhiyuan, Director of the Bureau.

Defendant: Qingdao Eastern Hospital, Shandong Province. Domicile: Donghai Middle Road, Qingdao. Legal Representative: Li Shihao, President of the Hospital.

Plaintiff Guo Zhonglian instituted an action in the Shibei District People's Court of Qingdao, Shandong Province, against defendants Qingdao Municipal Health Bureau (hereinafter referred to as the Municipal Health Bureau) and Qingdao Eastern Hospital (hereinafter referred to as Eastern Hospital) concerning a loan contract dispute.

Plaintiff's Allegations: Qingdao Meiyuan Hotel Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as Meiyuan Hotel) had previously entered into a contract with the plaintiff to borrow RMB 1.6 million (hereafter, unless otherwise noted, all monetary units are in RMB). Upon maturity, the loan was not repaid. Because the shareholders failed to make capital contributions in full, Meiyuan Hotel's business license was revoked by the administrative department for industry and commerce. The shareholder that failed to make capital contributions in full, Qingdao Sanatorium of the Nuclear Industry (hereinafter referred to as the Sanatorium) and its subordinate unit, the Labor Service Company of Qingdao Sanatorium of the Nuclear Industry (hereinafter referred to as the Service Company), have now been taken over by the Qingdao Municipal People's Government of Shandong Province (hereinafter referred to as the Municipal Government) and placed under the management of the defendant Municipal Health Bureau. The Municipal Health Bureau, using the assets of the Sanatorium and the Service Company, established the defendant Eastern Hospital. Since the two defendants have taken possession of the assets of the Sanatorium and the Service Company, they should settle their debts. The plaintiff requests that the court order the two defendants to repay the principal of RMB 1.6 million plus the corresponding interest.

The plaintiff submitted the following evidence:

1.  The Loan Agreement signed by Guo Zhonglian and Meiyuan Hotel on August 8, 1996;
2.  The transfer voucher showing Guo Zhonglian's transfer of RMB 1.6 million to Meiyuan Hotel on August 12, 1996;
3.  The receipt issued by Meiyuan Hotel to Guo Zhonglian on August 12, 1996, acknowledging receipt of RMB 1.6 million;
4.  A copy of the "Sino-American Cooperation to Establish 'Qingdao Meiyuan Hotel' Co., Ltd. Contract" signed by the former Sanatorium, the Service Company, and Xie Huadong on September 11, 1993;
5.  A copy of the "State-Owned Land Use Certificate" (Qing Guo Yong (94) Zi No. 00001) issued by the Qingdao Land Administration Bureau on January 5, 1994;
6.  A copy of the "Construction Land Planning Permit" (No. 95109) issued by the Qingdao Planning Bureau on August 8, 1995;
7.  A copy of the "Application Report on Changing the Planning Scheme for the Meiyuan Hotel Expansion Project" submitted by Meiyuan Hotel to the Qingdao Planning Bureau on September 10, 1996;
8.  The official letter issued by the Sanatorium to the Qingdao Planning Bureau on October 9, 1996;
9.  The "Request for Assistance to Stop All Approval Procedures for Meiyuan Hotel" (He Qing Liao (97) No. 50) issued by the Sanatorium to relevant departments of the Municipal Government on December 17, 1997;
10. The "Notice of Limited Time to Cancel Registration" issued by the Qingdao Administration for Industry and Commerce to Meiyuan Hotel on November 24, 1998;
11. A copy of the "Letter on Agreeing to Take Over the Sanatorium" issued by the Municipal Government on December 28, 1998;
12. The "Administrative Penalty Decision" (Qing Gong Shang Qi Jian Chu Zi (1999) No. 12) issued by the Qingdao Administration for Industry and Commerce on April 16, 1999;
13. A copy of the "Notice on Issues Concerning the Transfer of the Sanatorium to the Management of the Municipal Health Bureau" issued by the Municipal Government on August 11, 1999.

Defendant Municipal Health Bureau's Defense: This defendant is merely a subordinate agency of the Municipal Government, performing the duties of the health administration department pursuant to the Municipal Government's documents. This defendant is neither an independent legal person nor has it taken possession of the assets of the Sanatorium, and it has no connection whatsoever with the loan mentioned by the plaintiff. The plaintiff's claim against this defendant for repayment lacks factual and legal basis, and the claim should be dismissed.

Defendant Eastern Hospital's Defense: 1. Although the plaintiff's evidence shows that Meiyuan Hotel had previously borrowed funds, from the use of the borrowed funds, this loan was a personal loan by Xie Huadong, the legal representative of Meiyuan Hotel, and is unrelated to Meiyuan Hotel. 2. There is evidence showing that the plaintiff used part of this loan as his investment in Meiyuan Hotel and should not demand repayment of that portion from Meiyuan Hotel. 3. Pursuant to law, the liability of a shareholder who fails to make capital contributions is limited to the amount of the shortfall. In prior disputes related to this case, both the Sanatorium and the Service Company have already assumed civil liability and economic liability for their failure to make capital contributions to Meiyuan Hotel, and therefore the Sanatorium should not be held liable for any other obligations. 4. This defendant is a new legal person established pursuant to an administrative decision of the Municipal Government, not a successor to the Sanatorium, and is a different legal entity from the Sanatorium. The taking over of the Sanatorium's assets was an act of the Municipal Government, and the promise to discharge the Sanatorium's debts was also made by the Municipal Government and is unrelated to this defendant. This defendant is not obligated to assume the liability for repaying the Sanatorium's debts. The plaintiff's claim against this defendant for repayment lacks legal basis, and the claim should be dismissed.

The defendant Eastern Hospital submitted the following evidence:

1.  A copy of the receipt issued by Guo Zhonglian on April 25, 1997, acknowledging receipt of "one company seal of Xie Huadong's company";
2.  A copy of the "Decision on the Division of Work among Senior Management of the Company" made by Meiyuan Hotel on June 22, 1998, which states: "Mr. Guo Zhonglian is responsible for engineering and related matters";
3.  A copy of the "Notice on Issues Concerning the Transfer of the Sanatorium to the Management of the Municipal Health Bureau" issued by the Municipal Government on August 11, 1999;
4.  A copy of the "Medical Institution Practice License (Duplicate)" of Eastern Hospital;
5.  A copy of the minutes of the meeting with Xie Huadong on January 19, 2000, wherein Xie Huadong stated that he actually received RMB 1.6 million from Guo Zhonglian, that Guo Zhonglian used RMB 1 million of his loan as an investment in Meiyuan Hotel, that Guo Zhonglian's investment constituted 5% of the foreign party's investment, and that Guo Zhonglian served as a director on the foreign side; although Guo did not draw a salary, he frequently incurred expenses of RMB 10,000 to 20,000;
6.  A copy of the interrogation record of Guo Zhonglian by the Linqu County Public Security Bureau on December 10, 1998, wherein Guo Zhonglian stated that he actually lent RMB 1.6 million to Xie Huadong, and that Xie Huadong only agreed to give him 5% equity.

After examination and authentication of the evidence, the Shibei District People's Court of Qingdao found the following facts:

I. The Circumstances of the Loan in This Case

On August 8, 1996, plaintiff Guo Zhonglian and Meiyuan Hotel entered into a Loan Agreement, stipulating: Meiyuan Hotel would borrow RMB 2 million from Guo Zhonglian, with a term of one year and an annual interest rate of 20%, secured by the operating rights of Meiyuan Hotel's multi-functional hall; the annual interest of 20% amounting to RMB 400,000 would be deducted in one lump sum; the agreement would take effect when Guo Zhonglian transferred RMB 1.6 million into Meiyuan Hotel's account within three days from the date of signing; upon maturity, Meiyuan Hotel would repay RMB 2 million.

After the execution of this agreement, on August 12, 1996, plaintiff Guo Zhonglian transferred RMB 1.6 million to Meiyuan Hotel via bank transfer. On the same day, Meiyuan Hotel issued a receipt acknowledging receipt of RMB 1.6 million. On August 14, the funds were disbursed by Xie Huadong, the legal representative of Meiyuan Hotel. The loan has not been repaid to date.

II. The Establishment, Cancellation, and Revocation of Meiyuan Hotel

On September 11, 1993, the Service Company, a subordinate unit of the Sanatorium, and Xie Huadong, a Chinese-American, entered into a contract to jointly invest in and establish Meiyuan Hotel. The contract stipulated that the registered capital of Meiyuan Hotel would be USD 5 million, with total investment by both parties of USD 6.52 million. The Service Company's total contribution as the Chinese party was valued at USD 4.22 million, represented by the third sanatorium building and restaurant (valued at RMB 7.82 million) and the right to use 12,700 square meters of land surrounding the building (use price of RMB 16.51 million). Land use taxes to be levied annually by the government would be paid by Meiyuan Hotel. Xie Huadong signed the contract as a representative of American First Beverage and Food Company, but the contract did not bear the seal of that company.

On January 14, 1994, Meiyuan Hotel was established and registered as a Sino-foreign cooperative enterprise, with Xie Huadong as its legal representative. The term of the business license was from January 14, 1994, to January 13, 2024.

On April 4, 1994, the Qingdao Land Administration Bureau issued the "State-Owned Land Use Certificate" (Qing Guo Yong (94) Zi No. 00001), changing the user of the 12,700 square meters of land to Meiyuan Hotel. The certificate's term of use was from January 14, 1994, to January 13, 2024. Apart from this, the Service Company did not contribute the third sanatorium building and restaurant as capital to Meiyuan Hotel as agreed.

On October 9, 1996, the Sanatorium sent an official letter to the Qingdao Planning Bureau, stating: "Meiyuan Hotel is a star-rated foreign-related hotel established through cooperation between our Sanatorium and American First Beverage and Food Company. Our Sanatorium contributed real estate as capital. Because our Sanatorium's buildings could not meet the hotel's functional requirements, upon approval by the company's board of directors, renovation and expansion based on our Sanatorium's original buildings are planned. Meiyuan Hotel is now applying for the relevant construction approval procedures with your bureau."

On December 17, 1997, the Sanatorium issued the "Request for Assistance to Stop All Approval Procedures for Meiyuan Hotel" (He Qing Liao (97) No. 50) to relevant departments of the Municipal Government, stating: "Our Sanatorium signed a contract with Mr. Xie Huadong, a Chinese-American, in May 1993 to cooperate in establishing Meiyuan Hotel using the real estate of our third sanatorium area. Because the other party is fundamentally unable to invest and cooperate and has engaged in improper operations, the hotel has incurred enormous debts before its construction has even begun. Our Sanatorium is actively contacting the other party to resolve the contractual issues. To prevent further losses to the Chinese party, we request that the relevant government departments assist in suspending all processing of procedures for Meiyuan Hotel, until the other party introduces funds from abroad and compensates for the substantial losses already caused to the state. After that, our party and the other party will jointly submit applications."

On November 24, 1998, the Qingdao Administration for Industry and Commerce issued a "Notice of Limited Time to Cancel Registration" to Meiyuan Hotel, stating: "Upon investigation, your company's capital contributions have not been made within the prescribed time. You are hereby ordered to complete the cancellation procedures within three months. Failure to do so will result in handling according to relevant regulations."

On April 16, 1999, the Qingdao Administration for Industry and Commerce issued the "Administrative Penalty Decision" (Qing Gong Shang Qi Jian Chu Zi (1999) No. 12), stating: Because the joint venture parties of Meiyuan Hotel failed to pay their respective capital contributions in full by the time limit stipulated in the joint venture contract, Meiyuan Hotel's business license was revoked.

III. The Transfer, Takeover, and Establishment of Eastern Hospital

On December 28, 1998, the Municipal Government issued a "Letter on Agreeing to Take Over the Sanatorium" to China National Nuclear Corporation, stating: "Regarding the transfer of the Sanatorium to the management of our city, after study, our city agrees to take it over. The relevant matters are hereby communicated by letter as follows: 1. After the Sanatorium is transferred in its entirety to the management of our city, all land use rights and all property rights shall be assigned to our city, all personnel shall be received and resettled by our city, and the claims and debts shall also be assumed by our city. ..."

On August 11, 1999, the Municipal Government issued Qing Zheng Zi [1999] No. 38, the "Notice on Issues Concerning the Transfer of the Sanatorium to the Management of the Municipal Health Bureau," stating: "Pursuant to the 'Agreement between China National Nuclear Corporation and Qingdao Municipal People's Government on the Handover of the Sanatorium,' the Sanatorium was transferred in its entirety to our city on July 16, 1999. To further promote the implementation of regional health planning and meet the medical and health service needs of residents in the eastern new district, the Municipal Government has decided: to transfer the Sanatorium, after its transfer, in its established structure to the management of the Municipal Health Bureau, and on this basis, to establish Eastern Hospital. The relevant matters are hereby notified as follows: 1. The relevant departments of the city, including planning, finance, planning, health, state-owned asset management, and establishment, shall, according to the requirements of the 'Agreement between China National Nuclear Corporation and Qingdao Municipal People's Government on the Handover of the Sanatorium,' jointly complete the handover work. 2. The relevant departments of the city, including planning, finance, planning, and health, shall promptly study and put forward feasibility study opinions on establishing Eastern Hospital on the basis of the Sanatorium. 3. The Municipal Health Bureau shall earnestly conduct the ideological work among the cadres and staff of the Sanatorium to ensure the normal progress of all work. 4. The Municipal Health Bureau is responsible for the specific work of renovating and constructing Eastern Hospital, and the relevant departments shall actively provide support and cooperation to ensure that the project is completed on schedule and put into use as soon as possible."

On September 6, 1999, the defendant Municipal Health Bureau issued Qing Wei Fa [1999] No. 91, the "Notice on Taking Over the Sanatorium," stating: "Sanatorium, units subordinate to the Bureau: Pursuant to the Municipal Government's 'Notice on Issues Concerning the Transfer of the Sanatorium to the Management of the Municipal Health Bureau' and the Municipal Establishment Office's 'Reply on Rebuilding the Sanatorium into Eastern Hospital,' after study, the relevant matters are hereby notified as follows: 1. After the former Sanatorium is placed under the management of our Bureau, it shall be temporarily referred to as 'Qingdao Eastern Hospital,' and its official name will be determined after its renovation and expansion into a general hospital. 2. The establishment of Eastern Hospital shall be temporarily controlled at 304 persons (the current registered number), the number of hospital-level leadership positions shall be temporarily set at 3, and funding shall be provided by the city's fiscal deficit allocation. The 'three determinations' will be re-established after the general hospital is built. Eastern Hospital is requested to handle matters such as changing its official seal in accordance with this notice and, pursuant to the provisions of the 'Shandong Province Administrative Measures for the Registration of Public Institutions,' complete registration with the public institution registration authority within three months."

After the Sanatorium in its entire structure was placed under the management of the defendant Municipal Health Bureau, the defendant Eastern Hospital, established on this basis, continued to occupy the original site of the Sanatorium, used the same land and buildings as the Sanatorium, and the person in charge of Eastern Hospital was also the person in charge of the original Sanatorium.

On October 26, 1999, the defendant Eastern Hospital obtained a "Medical Institution Practice License."

IV. Prior Litigation Related to This Case

On July 20, 1999, plaintiff Guo Zhonglian filed an action against Meiyuan Hotel, the Sanatorium, and the Service Company, seeking repayment of the loan of RMB 1.6 million. The court found that Meiyuan Hotel's business license, as sued by Guo Zhonglian, had been revoked. The court then ruled to dismiss Guo Zhonglian's claim on the ground that the subject of the action did not comply with legal requirements.

The Shibei District People's Court of Qingdao held that:

I. Regarding the Nature of the Loan in Question

The Loan Agreement between plaintiff Guo Zhonglian and Meiyuan Hotel contained a clause stipulating: "Interest at 20% amounting to RMB 400,000 shall be deducted in one lump sum, and upon maturity, Meiyuan Hotel shall repay RMB 2 million." This clause violates legal provisions and shall be null and void. The other clauses represent the true intentions of the parties and do not violate mandatory provisions of the law; therefore, they are valid.

Article 8 of the Contract Law of the People's Republic of China provides: "A lawfully formed contract is legally binding on the parties. The parties shall perform their obligations as agreed and shall not alter or terminate the contract without authorization." After the loan agreement was signed, plaintiff Guo Zhonglian performed the obligation of payment as agreed, and Meiyuan Hotel issued a receipt acknowledging receipt of RMB 1.6 million. This loan shall be determined as a loan from Guo Zhonglian to Meiyuan Hotel. Although defendant Eastern Hospital asserts that this loan was a personal loan to Xie Huadong, it failed to submit corresponding evidence; therefore, this assertion is not adopted.

II. Whether Part of the Loan Was Converted into an Investment

Defendant Eastern Hospital, relying on evidence 1, 2, 5, and 6 submitted by it, argues that plaintiff Guo Zhonglian converted RMB 1 million of his loan into an investment in Meiyuan Hotel, and as a result served as a director on the foreign side of Meiyuan Hotel, was responsible for engineering and related matters, although not drawing a salary, frequently incurred expenses of RMB 10,000 to 20,000, and held the company's seal, and therefore should not be entitled to demand repayment of this portion of the investment.

Plaintiff Guo Zhonglian alleges that he never converted the loan into an investment, never received 5% equity in Meiyuan Hotel, was unaware of the decision on the division of work among Meiyuan Hotel's senior management, and that the seal he received was only a company seal of an American company given to him by Xie Huadong, not the seal of Meiyuan Hotel.

Upon investigation, when Meiyuan Hotel failed to repay the loan on time, Xie Huadong, the legal representative of Meiyuan Hotel, did have an intention to treat RMB 1 million of plaintiff Guo Zhonglian's loan as an investment in Meiyuan Hotel. However, because this was not agreed to by Guo Zhonglian, it was not implemented, and the RMB 1 million remained a loan.

III. Whether the Sanatorium and the Service Company Should Bear Liability for Repayment of the Loan in This Case

Meiyuan Hotel was a Sino-foreign cooperative enterprise. The liquidating entities of such an enterprise are the joint venture parties. The evidence submitted by plaintiff Guo Zhonglian can confirm that the Sanatorium and the Service Company failed to contribute the third sanatorium building and restaurant, valued at RMB 7.82 million, as capital to Meiyuan Hotel as agreed, and therefore should bear the liquidation liability for the failure to make capital contributions in full. Defendant Eastern Hospital asserts that the Sanatorium and the Service Company have already assumed civil and economic liability for the failure to make capital contributions, but it fails to provide corresponding evidence to support this assertion; therefore, this assertion is not adopted.

IV. Whether the Defendant Municipal Health Bureau Should Repay the Debts of the Sanatorium

The defendant Municipal Health Bureau is not an independent legal person. It lacks the capacity to participate in civil activities such as investment, operation, and profit-making, and cannot take possession of the assets of the original Sanatorium. The Municipal Health Bureau, as a subordinate agency of the Municipal Government, taking over and managing the Sanatorium and establishing the defendant Eastern Hospital on that basis, is an administrative act of performing its functions as a government department. Plaintiff Guo Zhonglian has no evidence to prove that the Municipal Health Bureau, in the course of performing its duties, took possession of or used the assets of the Sanatorium. He cannot, on the ground that the Municipal Government once promised to repay debts, request the Municipal Health Bureau to perform the civil liability of repayment.

V. Whether the Defendant Eastern Hospital Should Repay the Debts of the Sanatorium

The defendant Eastern Hospital was indeed established by an administrative decision of the Municipal Government. However, it should be noted that the Municipal Government made this administrative decision on the basis of taking over the assets of the Sanatorium and assuming the Sanatorium's claims and debts. The assets of the Sanatorium taken over by the Municipal Government were all used to establish Eastern Hospital. This portion of assets constitutes the economic foundation for Eastern Hospital to become a new legal person. The Municipal Government's promise to assume the Sanatorium's claims and debts should, as a matter of course, be fulfilled by Eastern Hospital. Eastern Hospital's defense that the Sanatorium's assets were taken over by the Municipal Government and that the Sanatorium's claims and debts were also promised to be assumed by the Municipal Government, all of which are unrelated to it, cannot stand. Eastern Hospital shall repay the debts of the Sanatorium.

Article 90 of the *General Principles of the Civil Law of the PRC* provides: "Lawful lending relationships shall be protected by law." Article 108 provides: "Debts shall be repaid. If a debtor is temporarily unable to repay the debt, the debtor may repay in installments with the consent of the creditor or a ruling by a people's court. If a debtor is able to repay but refuses to do so, the people's court shall order compulsory repayment." Meiyuan Hotel owes the plaintiff, Guo Zhonglian, RMB 1.6 million, which must be repaid. The Nuclear Sanatorium failed to make a full capital contribution when Meiyuan Hotel was established. In accordance with the law, it shall bear corresponding civil liability within the limit of RMB 7.82 million for the deficient capital contribution. After the Nuclear Sanatorium was taken over by the municipal government, its assets were received by the defendant, Eastern Hospital, which is obligated to repay the debts of the Nuclear Sanatorium. Guo Zhonglian's request for an order that Eastern Hospital repay his debt and compensate for corresponding interest losses is justified and should be supported; his request for an order that the defendant, the Municipal Health Bureau, bear the liability for repayment in this case is not supported due to failure to provide corresponding evidence. Accordingly, the Shibei District People's Court of Qingdao rendered the following judgment on March 27, 2002:

1. The defendant, Eastern Hospital, shall repay the loan of RMB 1.6 million to the plaintiff, Guo Zhonglian.

2. The defendant, Eastern Hospital, shall pay the plaintiff, Guo Zhonglian, interest losses on the aforesaid loan of RMB 1.6 million from August 9, 1997, to the date of confirmed payment (interest calculated at the loan interest rate of the People's Bank of China for the corresponding period).

The first and second items above shall be fulfilled by the defendant, Eastern Hospital, within ten days after this judgment takes legal effect.

3. The plaintiff, Guo Zhonglian's, claims against the defendant, the Municipal Health Bureau, are dismissed.

Court acceptance fee of RMB 18,110 shall be borne by the defendant, Eastern Hospital.

After the judgment was pronounced, the defendant, Eastern Hospital, being dissatisfied, appealed to the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province. The reasons for appeal were: 1. There is no relationship of inheritance of creditor's rights and debts between the appellant and the Nuclear Sanatorium, and the appellant has no obligation to repay the debts of the Nuclear Sanatorium. 2. The Nuclear Sanatorium was not a shareholder of Meiyuan Hotel, and there is no issue of deficient capital contribution by the Nuclear Sanatorium to Meiyuan Hotel. The actual Chinese shareholder of Meiyuan Hotel should be the Nuclear Labor Service, and the Nuclear Labor Service and the Nuclear Sanatorium are two separate legal persons.

Upon trial, the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court of Shandong Province confirmed the facts found and determined in the first instance as true.

The Qingdao Intermediate People's Court held that the fact that the appellee, Guo Zhonglian, lent RMB 1.6 million to Meiyuan Hotel is true, and Meiyuan Hotel shall bear the liability for repayment. The focus of the dispute in this appeal is whether the Nuclear Sanatorium was a shareholder of Meiyuan Hotel, and whether the appellant should bear the liability for repayment due to the deficient capital contribution of the Nuclear Sanatorium.

Although Meiyuan Hotel was established pursuant to the Sino-foreign cooperative contract signed between the Nuclear Labor Service and the First Beverage and Food Company of the United States, the capital contribution agreed upon by the Nuclear Labor Service in the contract was the real estate owned by the Nuclear Sanatorium. During the performance of the contract, the Nuclear Sanatorium came forward to invest its real estate. The procedures required during the establishment of Meiyuan Hotel were also issued by the Nuclear Sanatorium in its own name. In relevant correspondence issued by the Nuclear Sanatorium to the Qingdao Municipal Planning Bureau and other departments, it stated that Meiyuan Hotel was established through investment by the Nuclear Sanatorium and the First Beverage and Food Company of the United States. In light of the above facts and the subordinate relationship between the Nuclear Labor Service and the Nuclear Sanatorium, it can be determined that the actual Chinese investor of Meiyuan Hotel was the Nuclear Sanatorium. As the genuine Chinese shareholder of Meiyuan Hotel, the Nuclear Sanatorium failed to make a full capital contribution and, after Meiyuan Hotel's business license was revoked, failed to promptly liquidate the hotel's creditor's rights and debts. Such conduct harmed the interests of Meiyuan Hotel's lawful creditors and should bear civil liability. Since the Nuclear Sanatorium no longer exists, the appellant, Eastern Hospital, was established on the basis of the former Nuclear Sanatorium. All assets (including the real estate that should have been invested in Meiyuan Hotel) and the personnel on the roster of the former Nuclear Sanatorium have been taken over by Eastern Hospital. Therefore, Eastern Hospital shall bear the liability for repaying the debts of the former Nuclear Sanatorium.

In summary, the original judgment was correct and should be affirmed. The appellant, Eastern Hospital's, grounds for appeal are insufficient and should be dismissed. Accordingly, the Qingdao Intermediate People's Court, pursuant to Article 152 and Article 153, Paragraph 1, Item (1) of the *Civil Procedure Law of the PRC*, rendered the following judgment on March 10, 2003:

The appeal is dismissed and the original judgment is affirmed.

Court acceptance fee of RMB 18,110 for the second instance shall be borne by the appellant, Eastern Hospital.
